The '''Blue Dog Coalition''' (commonly known as the '''Blue Dogs''' or '''Blue Dog Democrats''') is a [[Congressional caucus|caucus]] in the [[United States House of Representatives]] comprising [[centrist]] members from the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]].<ref name="U.S. House has fewer moderate Democrats"/><ref name="auto"/><ref name="auto1"/> The caucus was founded as a group of [[Conservative Democrat|conservative Democrats]] in 1995 in response to defeats in the [[1994 United States elections|1994 elections]]. Historically, Blue Dog Coalition has been [[Fiscal conservatism|fiscally]] and [[Social conservatism in the United States|socially]] [[Conservative Democrat|conservative]], representing the [[center-right]] in the Democratic Party. However, the current Blue Dog Coalition  remains the most conservative grouping of Democrats in the House, broadly adopting [[social liberalism|socially liberal]] and [[fiscal conservatism|fiscally conservative]] policies and promoting fiscal restraint.<ref name="Kane">{{cite news |last1=Kane |first1=Paul |date=19 February 2020 |title=Blue Dog Democrats celebrate a milestone but stand alone on a core issue — fiscal restraint |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/powerpost/blue-dog-democrats-celebrate-a-milestone-but-stand-alone-on-a-core-issue--fiscal-restraint/2020/02/19/456b6310-533d-11ea-9e47-59804be1dcfb_story.html |access-date=19 July 2022 |newspaper=[[Washington Post]]}}</ref>

The caucus has 19 members.<ref name = "members">{{cite web |url=https://bluedogcaucus-costa.house.gov/members |title = Blue Dog Coalition {{!}} Bold Leadership. Common Sense Solutions}}</ref> The co-chairs of the Blue Dog Coalition for the 117th Congress are U.S. representatives [[Ed Case]], [[Stephanie Murphy]], and [[Tom O'Halleran]]. The chair of the Blue Dog PAC, the Coalition's political organization, is U.S. representative [[Kurt Schrader]], who lost renomination in 2022.

